Aim: To integrate Docker with Kubernetes and deploy a containerized application using Kubernetes.

Requirements:
- Docker Desktop
- Kubernetes
- kubectl
- Java application from Cycle 1, Exercise 7
Step 1: Start Docker
Open Docker Desktop and make sure Docker is running.
Step 2: Enable Kubernetes
1. Open Docker Desktop.
2. Go to Settings.
3. Select Kubernetes.
4. Enable Kubernetes.
5. Click Apply and Restart.
If your lab uses Minikube instead, use the Minikube commands below rather than enabling Docker Desktop Kubernetes.
Step 3: Check Kubernetes installation
Open terminal and execute:
kubectl version --client


Check cluster information:
kubectl cluster-info


Check nodes:
kubectl get nodes


Expected output:
NAME             STATUS   ROLES
docker-desktop   Ready    control-plane


Step 4: Build Docker image
Open your Exercise 7 project directory.
mvn clean package


Build the Docker image:
docker build -t user-registration:latest .


Check the image:
docker images


Step 5: Create a Kubernetes Deployment
Create a file named deployment.yaml.

deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: user-registration

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: user-registration

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: user-registration

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: registration-container
          image: user-registration:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080


Step 6: Apply the deployment
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml


Output:
deployment.apps/user-registration created


Step 7: Check the pods
kubectl get pods


Expected output:
NAME                                 READY   STATUS
user-registration-xxxxxx-abcde      1/1     Running
user-registration-xxxxxx-fghij      1/1     Running


Step 8: Expose the application
Create another file named service.yaml.

service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service

metadata:
  name: registration-service

spec:
  type: NodePort

  selector:
    app: user-registration

  ports:
    - port: 8080
      targetPort: 8080
      nodePort: 30080


Apply the service:
kubectl apply -f service.yaml


Output:
service/registration-service created


Step 9: Check the service
kubectl get services


Expected output:
NAME                   TYPE       PORT(S)
registration-service   NodePort   8080:30080/TCP


For Docker Desktop Kubernetes, test using:
http://localhost:30080


For a Minikube cluster, you can use:
minikube service registration-service --url


Step 10: Delete the deployment
kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml


Delete the service:
kubectl delete -f service.yaml


Result: Successfully integrated Docker with Kubernetes and deployed a containerized Java application using Kubernetes Deployment and Service.
